From 60e9308583214b20920f0aec4db6d905e365c0d8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yushuailong <yyyusl@qq.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 21:41:34 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] fs/fat: Truncate the live chain when opening with O_TRUNC.

An open file may have allocated clusters without syncing its start
cluster to the directory entry. Opening the same file with O_TRUNC
must release that live chain rather than the stale on-disk chain.

Copy the shared start cluster into the directory entry before calling
fat_dirtruncate(), as fat_truncate() already does. This prevents lost
clusters while preserving the shared size and cursor updates.

Validated unsynced three-cluster files through two handles on FAT12,
FAT16 and FAT32 RAM disks, including raw FAT and remount checks.

Assisted-by: Codex:GPT-6
Signed-off-by: yushuailong <yyyusl@qq.com>
---
 fs/fat/fs_fat32.c | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/fs/fat/fs_fat32.c b/fs/fat/fs_fat32.c
index 7badc47e02bb4..d54e970f41637 100644
--- a/fs/fat/fs_fat32.c
+++ b/fs/fat/fs_fat32.c
@@ -553,6 +553,12 @@ static int fat_open(FAR struct file *filep, FAR const char *relpath,
                 {
                   goto errout_with_lock;
                 }
+
+              /* The directory entry may not describe the live chain yet. */
+
+              DIR_PUTFSTCLUSTHI(direntry, truncshared->s_startcluster >> 16);
+              DIR_PUTFSTCLUSTLO(direntry,
+                               truncshared->s_startcluster & 0xffff);
             }
 
           ret = fat_dirtruncate(fs, direntry);
